-- Invite only is a badge on a calendar entry, not a lock on it.
--
-- Since 20260730113925 the `calendar_events` read policies hid an invite-only
-- date (marked itself, or generated by a weekly entry marked invite only) from
-- everybody but managers and the people with an RSVP row for it. Nothing in the
-- product could ever write that row for somebody else, so in practice ticking
-- "invite only" hid the class from every member, while the manager screen's
-- own help text said it was only a label. The club decided on 2026-10-30 that
-- it is only a label: the entry shows its badge and stays visible to everybody
-- its `visibility` allows. `visibility` (public / members) is the one setting
-- that restricts who sees an entry, and it is untouched here.
--
-- The app stopped hiding invite-only dates in the same change (the calendar
-- page and the personal calendar links read through the service role and
-- applied the rule in code). This removes the database's half, so a direct
-- read with the publishable key answers the same as the site does.
--
-- Loosening only: a date that was readable stays readable, and an invite-only
-- date becomes readable to exactly the people its visibility already admits.
-- No code depends on these policies or the two helpers dropped below, so this
-- can go live before or after the code that stopped using them.

-- ---------- the policies, without the invite-only clause ----------
-- Each predicate is the one 20260802000000 created, with the invite-only
-- condition removed and nothing else changed.
DROP POLICY IF EXISTS "Anyone can read public events" ON public.calendar_events;
CREATE POLICY "Anyone can read public events"
ON public.calendar_events
FOR SELECT
USING (visibility = 'public');

DROP POLICY IF EXISTS "Paid members can read members-only events" ON public.calendar_events;
CREATE POLICY "Paid members can read members-only events"
ON public.calendar_events
FOR SELECT
TO authenticated
USING (
  public.has_role(auth.uid(), 'manager'::app_role)
  OR public.has_active_paid_membership(auth.uid())
  OR visibility = 'public'
);

-- ---------- the helpers only those clauses called ----------
-- After the policies above, nothing references either function. No CASCADE on
-- purpose: if the live database holds a policy using one of them that this
-- repo does not know about, the DROP must fail loudly rather than take that
-- policy with it.
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS private.event_is_invite_only(uuid);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS private.is_event_invitee(uuid, uuid);

-- `anon`'s USAGE on the private schema goes too, as defence in depth. It can
-- break no read: a policy calls its helper by OID, so only EXECUTE on the
-- function is ever checked, and nothing left in `private` is granted to `anon`
-- (is_commenter_blocked and waiver_pdf_readable_by_caller are `authenticated`
-- only, run_notification_digest runs as the cron owner). USAGE gates only calls
-- by name. `authenticated` keeps its USAGE; see docs/database.md.
REVOKE USAGE ON SCHEMA private FROM anon;

NOTIFY pgrst, 'reload schema';
